<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Masjid al-Haram</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Great Mosque of Mecca, Sacred Mosque</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Masjid al-Haram, the Grand Mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a>, is the holiest site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, surrounding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaaba">Kaaba</a> towards which Muslims pray. It is the centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">hajj</a> pilgrimage, and Saudi rulers have enlarged it repeatedly since the 1950s to hold millions of worshippers. In November 1979 armed militants led by Juhayman al-Otaybi seized the mosque, and the two-week siege that followed led the Saudi government to adopt stricter religious policies.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/masjid-al-haram.jpg" alt="Masjid al-Haram" width="320" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Saudipics.com · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edited_Great_Mosque_of_Mecca1_5-2019-ccsa4.0_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
